# Gate a Release

This notebook decides whether to promote a tuned routing policy. You search
for a winner, run the baseline and the winner on the same held-out cases with
`Tune.compare/3`, and apply a release rule that requires evidence for one
critical case. The fixture is built so the aggregate score rises while the
critical case regresses. The router is plain Elixir code, so the notebook runs
offline with no credentials and no model calls.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"}
])
```

## Define the router

The configuration selects a routing policy. The baseline sends card fraud to
`risk-review` and everything else to `general`. The tuned policy sends routine
tickets to `billing`, but it also sends card fraud to `general`.

```elixir
defmodule Router do
  @moduledoc "Routes support tickets with a configurable policy."

  def route(%{policy: :baseline}, :card_fraud), do: "risk-review"
  def route(%{policy: :baseline}, _ticket), do: "general"
  def route(%{policy: :tuned}, :card_fraud), do: "general"
  def route(%{policy: :tuned}, _ticket), do: "billing"
end
```

## Find a search winner

Search the policy with the `:grid` strategy on validation data. The validation
split holds only a routine ticket, so the tuned policy wins.

```elixir
route_score = fn tune_case, output -> if output == tune_case.expected, do: 1, else: 0 end
baseline = %{policy: :baseline}

{:ok, winner} =
  Tune.new(baseline, run: &Router.route/2)
  |> Tune.data(validation: [%{id: "invoice", input: :invoice_copy, expected: "billing"}])
  |> Tune.vary(policy: [:baseline, :tuned])
  |> Tune.score(:route, route_score)
  |> Tune.search(:grid)
  |> Tune.tune()

winner.value
```

```text
%{policy: :tuned}
```

## Compare on held-out cases

Build a second pipeline for the held-out cases. Give each case a unique `id`:
`Tune.compare/3` pairs rows by ID and rejects duplicate or blank IDs before it
runs either target.

```elixir
holdout = [
  %{id: "card-fraud", input: :card_fraud, expected: "risk-review"},
  %{id: "refund", input: :refund, expected: "billing"},
  %{id: "invoice-copy", input: :invoice_copy, expected: "billing"}
]

paired_study = fn run, cases ->
  Tune.new(baseline, run: run)
  |> Tune.data(test: cases)
  |> Tune.score(:route, route_score)
end

{:ok, paired} = Tune.compare(paired_study.(&Router.route/2, holdout), winner, split: :test)

route = paired.metrics.route

IO.puts(
  "#{route.status}: #{Float.round(route.baseline_mean, 2)} -> " <>
    "#{Float.round(route.challenger_mean, 2)}, paired #{route.paired_count} of " <>
    "#{length(paired.case_ids)}, complete #{paired.complete?}"
)
```

```text
improved: 0.33 -> 0.67, paired 3 of 3, complete true
```

`Tune.compare/3` runs the original target and the winner on the same split.
The result is a `Tune.Comparison`. `paired_count` counts rows where both
targets have a score. Compare it with the length of `case_ids` to check
coverage. The aggregate improved by one third.

## Inspect the critical row

`rows` is keyed by case ID. Each metric has the baseline score, the challenger
score, the delta, and a status.

```elixir
paired.rows["card-fraud"].metrics.route
```

```text
%{status: :regressed, delta: -1, baseline: 1, challenger: 0}
```

The winner sends card fraud to the wrong queue. A rule that reads only the
aggregate would promote it.

## Write the release rule

The release rule is application code. This one holds the release when the
comparison is incomplete, when the critical row regresses, or when the
critical row has no paired score. It promotes only when the critical row is
tied or improved and the aggregate improved.

```elixir
defmodule ReleaseRule do
  @moduledoc "Requires paired evidence for a critical case before promotion."

  def decide(%{complete?: false}, _critical_id), do: :hold_incomplete

  def decide(paired, critical_id) do
    case get_in(paired.rows, [critical_id, :metrics, :route, :status]) do
      :regressed -> :hold_critical_regression
      status when status in [:tied, :improved] -> aggregate(paired)
      _missing -> :hold_missing_critical_evidence
    end
  end

  defp aggregate(paired) do
    if paired.metrics.route.status == :improved, do: :promote, else: :hold
  end
end

ReleaseRule.decide(paired, "card-fraud")
```

```text
:hold_critical_regression
```

## Handle missing and failed evidence

A critical row can lack evidence in three ways. The rule holds the release in
each case.

When the winner's run fails on the critical case, the row status is `:error`.
The other rows still pair, so the aggregate looks better than before.

```elixir
failing = fn
  %{policy: :tuned}, :card_fraud -> {:error, :rules_service_unavailable}
  config, ticket -> Router.route(config, ticket)
end

{:ok, failed} = Tune.compare(paired_study.(failing, holdout), winner, split: :test)

{failed.rows["card-fraud"].metrics.route.status, failed.metrics.route.delta,
 failed.metrics.route.paired_count, ReleaseRule.decide(failed, "card-fraud")}
```

```text
{:error, 1.0, 2, :hold_missing_critical_evidence}
```

When the held-out set leaves out the critical case, every supplied row pairs,
but the rule finds no row for `card-fraud`.

```elixir
[_critical | routine] = holdout
{:ok, partial} = Tune.compare(paired_study.(&Router.route/2, routine), winner, split: :test)

{partial.metrics.route.paired_count, length(partial.case_ids),
 ReleaseRule.decide(partial, "card-fraud")}
```

```text
{2, 2, :hold_missing_critical_evidence}
```

When a limit stops the comparison, `complete?` is `false`. A deadline of zero
stops it before Tune reads the cases.

```elixir
expired = paired_study.(&Router.route/2, holdout) |> Tune.limit(deadline: 0)
{:ok, stopped} = Tune.compare(expired, winner, split: :test)

{stopped.case_ids, stopped.complete?, ReleaseRule.decide(stopped, "card-fraud")}
```

```text
{[], false, :hold_incomplete}
```

## Promote a safe winner

A winner that keeps card fraud in `risk-review` and improves routine tickets
passes the rule.

```elixir
safe = fn
  %{policy: :tuned}, :card_fraud -> "risk-review"
  config, ticket -> Router.route(config, ticket)
end

{:ok, passing} = Tune.compare(paired_study.(safe, holdout), winner, split: :test)

{passing.rows["card-fraud"].metrics.route.status, ReleaseRule.decide(passing, "card-fraud")}
```

```text
{:tied, :promote}
```

